{"subjectId":"sql","id":"semi-anti-joins","title":"Semi & Anti Joins (EXISTS, IN, NOT EXISTS)","group":"Joins & Set Operations","order":30,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <strong>semi join</strong> keeps rows that <em>have</em> a match; an <strong>anti join</strong> keeps rows that have <em>no</em> match. Neither returns columns from the other table, and neither duplicates rows.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-user-check\"></i>Semi join: customers who ordered</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT c.name FROM customers c\nWHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id)\nORDER BY c.name;\n\nSELECT c.name FROM customers c\nWHERE c.customer_id IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders)\nORDER BY c.name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n------\nAnanya\nImran\nRahul\nSneha\n\nname\n------\nAnanya\nImran\nRahul\nSneha</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Compare with a plain join, which returns a customer once <strong>per order</strong>:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT c.name FROM customers c\nJOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nORDER BY c.name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n------\nAnanya\nAnanya\nAnanya\nImran\nRahul\nRahul\nSneha\nSneha</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>EXISTS</code> stops at the first match and never multiplies rows. Using <code>JOIN</code> + <code>DISTINCT</code> to get the same list does more work and is a code smell.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-user-x\"></i>Anti join: customers who never ordered</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- 1. NOT EXISTS (recommended)\nSELECT c.name FROM customers c\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id);\n\n-- 2. LEFT JOIN … IS NULL\nSELECT c.name FROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nWHERE o.order_id IS NULL;\n\n-- 3. NOT IN (only safe if the subquery can never return NULL)\nSELECT name FROM customers\nWHERE customer_id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n-----\nKavya\n\nname\n-----\nKavya\n\nname\n-----\nKavya</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- products never sold\nSELECT p.name FROM products p\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM order_items oi WHERE oi.product_id = p.product_id);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n-------\nMonitor</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>The NOT IN + NULL trap, for real</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- departments that no employee belongs to…\nSELECT name FROM departments\nWHERE dept_id NOT IN (SELECT dept_id FROM employees);          -- Dev's NULL dept_id → empty!\n\nSELECT name FROM departments d\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM employees e WHERE e.dept_id = d.dept_id);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n----\n\nname\n---------\nMarketing</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Marketing should be returned, but <code>NOT IN</code> returned nothing because the subquery contains a <code>NULL</code>. <code>NOT EXISTS</code> is immune. If you must use <code>NOT IN</code>, add <code>WHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL</code> inside the subquery.</p></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Handles NULL</th><th>Duplicates</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>EXISTS</code> / <code>NOT EXISTS</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Never</td><td>Clearest intent; optimizers turn it into a semi/anti join</td></tr><tr><td><code>IN (subquery)</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Never</td><td>Fine for semi joins</td></tr><tr><td><code>NOT IN (subquery)</code></td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>Never</td><td>Returns nothing if the subquery has a <code>NULL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>LEFT JOIN … IS NULL</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Never (anti)</td><td>Common, portable; test a non-nullable column</td></tr><tr><td><code>JOIN</code> + <code>DISTINCT</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Removed afterwards</td><td>Extra work; avoid for existence checks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>EXISTS vs IN — which is faster?</summary><div><p>Modern optimizers (PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle, MySQL 8) usually rewrite both into the same semi join, so pick the clearest. The real difference is <code>NOT IN</code> vs <code>NOT EXISTS</code>: <code>NOT IN</code> gives wrong (empty) results when the subquery returns a <code>NULL</code>, so prefer <code>NOT EXISTS</code> for anti joins.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Find customers who have never placed an order.</summary><div><p><code>SELECT c.* FROM customers c WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id);</code> — or <code>LEFT JOIN orders o ON … WHERE o.order_id IS NULL</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}